 119 | ## Section 2 — Partial / Needs Follow-Up
 120 | 
 121 | ### ⚠️ Provider List Redundancy — core/router.py Tiers vs ninagate/providers.json
 122 | **Original finding:** `PROVIDERS_TIER1/2/3` in `router.py` and `ninagate/providers.json` represent the same data in two places. If one is edited without the other, models drift silently.
 123 | **What was done:** Today's commit unified the model name in both, but the structural duplication was NOT removed.
 124 | **Gap:** Two sources of truth still exist. No single canonical provider registry.
 125 | **Priority:** 🟠 TODO-P2
 126 | 
 127 | **Jules spec when ready:**
 128 | ```
 129 | File: core/router.py + ninagate/providers.json + ninagate/main.py
 130 | Goal: Make ninagate/providers.json the SINGLE canonical provider registry.
 131 |   - router.py should import/load provider definitions from providers.json at startup
 132 |     instead of defining PROVIDERS_TIER1/2/3 as hardcoded dicts
 133 |   - ninagate/main.py already reads providers.json — no change needed there
 134 |   - router.py: remove hardcoded tier dicts; add a loader that reads providers.json
 135 |     and builds tiers from a "tier" field in each provider entry
 136 |   - Add "tier": 1|2|3 to each entry in providers.json
 137 |   - Do NOT change routing logic, only the data source
 138 |   - Do NOT touch .env, guardian_engine.py, interfaces/, or tests/
 139 |   - Acceptance: python3 -c "from core.router import Router; r=Router(); print(r.ALL_PROVIDERS)" succeeds
 140 | ```
